        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.305,
            0.115,
            0.691,
            0.143
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "End-of-Chapter Exercises"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.161,
            0.881,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": "1. Compute the following using the improved version of Euclid’s algorithm."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.203,
            0.394,
            0.224
          ],
          "content": "(i) gcd(375, 825)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.226,
            0.443,
            0.247
          ],
          "content": "(ii) gcd(51000, 81000)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.249,
            0.479,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "(iii) gcd(1789287, 237656)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.272,
            0.479,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": "(iv) gcd(2587392, 157656)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.297,
            0.882,
            0.336
          ],
          "content": "2. Assume that $m \\geq n .$ Verify that d divides m and n if and only if d divides both n and m mod n."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.34,
            0.88,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "3. Write an algorithm prime(n) to check if n is prime. (**Hint:** A prime number p has exactly two distinct factors, 1 and p. Can you make use of divisors(n) to write out prime(n)?)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.402,
            0.882,
            0.462
          ],
          "content": "\\*4. Write an algorithm primedivisors(n) to compute the list of divisors of n that are prime numbers. (**Hint:** Compute divisors(n) and then filter out the primes in this list.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.465,
            0.881,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": "\\*5. We can also find the gcd of two numbers by computing prime factorisation of both the numbers. Try to write an algorithm to compute the prime factorisation of a number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.525,
            0.881,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": "(i) The prime factorisation of 180 is $2 ^ { 2 } \\times 3 ^ { 2 } \\times 5 ^ { 1 }$ . How would you represent this?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.19,
            0.568,
            0.881,
            0.608
          ],
          "content": "(ii) How would you compare the prime factorisations of two numbers?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.371,
            0.635,
            0.628,
            0.658
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Chapter Summary"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.674,
            0.817,
            0.695
          ],
          "content": "• An **algorithm** is a systematic procedure to solve a problem."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.699,
            0.85,
            0.72
          ],
          "content": "• Algorithms consist of basic steps that we know how to execute."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.724,
            0.881,
            0.766
          ],
          "content": "We may execute the same step a number of times; for instance, for each j in 1, 2, ..., n, check if j is a divisor of n."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.77,
            0.881,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": "A step in an algorithm may be conditional; for instance, add j to the list of divisors of n only if j divides n."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.816,
            0.881,
            0.856
          ],
          "content": "When we describe an algorithm to solve a problem, we need to justify that it is correct."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.86,
            0.881,
            0.9
          ],
          "content": "We can also analyse how many steps an algorithm takes, in terms of the size of its input, and decide how efficient it is."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
